      "4": {
        "class_type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline",
        "inputs": {
          "value": "TAIGA-02: roughly two-metre-long unmanned Russian planetary-exploration-inspired Mars rover. Squat cylindrical brushed-silver instrument housing with a closed flat top on a rectangular dark olive-green chassis. Four broad solid-metal wheels, two per side, with raised chevron ribs and dark hubs. Each wheel connects visibly to a sturdy short suspension arm. A short camera post sits at the FRONT edge of the circular housing with one dark lens block. One robust two-joint drilling arm mounts at the FRONT centre of the chassis, ending in a short cylindrical drill head, folded against the front for travel. Rear is a plain olive service panel. Restrained housing seams, recessed fasteners, matte green paint and enclosed dark joints. Scientific field instrument, no weaponry, cabin or ornamental antennas."
        },
        "_meta": {
          "title": "Vehicle description"
        }
      },
      "5": {
        "class_type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline",
        "inputs": {
          "value": "The reference images show the same vehicle on a studio background. Use them for vehicle identity only. Apply the requested action and weathering without redesigning the vehicle.\nTAIGA after three Earth years, still operating on a broad dry valley floor. Rear-right photograph preserves its circular silver housing and square green undercarriage, front drill folded on the far side. Red dust fills housing seams and wheel ribs. Olive paint is faded and locally scuffed on lower edges; silver surfaces dulled by abrasion, not mirror polished. Suspension and camera intact. A recent wheel trail curves between scattered stones. Low stratified escarpment recedes into a dusty horizon. Soft available afternoon light and ordinary expedition-camera framing, enduring scientific machine rather than cinematic wreck."
        },
        "_meta": {
          "title": "Scene description"
        }
      },
      "6": {
        "class_type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline",
        "inputs": {
          "value": "Photograph of a tangible full-size robotic vehicle, not a miniature. Restrained saturation, natural tonal transitions, fine material texture. Matte painted panels, subdued brushed-metal reflections, small dark sensor lenses. A few recessed fasteners, narrow panel seams and protected pivots at plausible scale. Every instrument is securely mounted; each arm has one continuous chain of connected joints. Suspension and propulsion have clearance to move. Sealed opaque equipment housings, no cabin or passenger windows. Preserve silhouette, equipment placement and propulsion type. No glossy toy finish, theatrical bloom, captions or graphic overlays.\n\nBarren Martian terrain: fine rusty ochre dust, dark basalt fragments, weathered layered outcrops, dry gravel and a distant eroded horizon under a muted dusty peach sky. No plants, cities, open water or people. Believable terrain scale and distant atmospheric haze.\n\nDocumentary photograph taken by a nearby robotic survey camera, ordinary 40mm-equivalent field of view at vehicle-body height. Available sunlight and sky fill, plausible directional shadows, restrained exposure, no studio lighting or theatrical rim light. Granular soil, irregular stones and fine dust in panel recesses, without excessive sharpening. Terrain occupies part of the frame to establish scale. Replace the white reference background entirely with the outdoor environment and relight the vehicle to match, not a pasted studio cutout."
        },
        "_meta": {
          "title": "Shared realism and photography"
        }
      },
